<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Determining Arc length-Q1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine the length of \( \displaystyle {@expr@}\), \({@lower_y@}\leq y\leq{@upper_y@}\).</p>
<p><em>Note: Round off your answer correct to 2 decimal places.</em></p>
<p>\(L=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution:</h3>
<p>First we differentiate the function with respect to \(x\) and then plug it into the \(ds\) formula, given by \( \displaystyle ds=\sqrt{1+[\frac{dy}{dx}]^{2}}dx\)
</p><br>
<p> \({@expr@}\). Then \(\displaystyle \frac{dy}{dx}={@dy@} \) where \({@lower_y@} \leq y \leq {@upper_y@} \) </p>
<p>
    \(
    \begin{align}
\displaystyle   ds=\sqrt{1+[\frac{dy}{dx}]^{2}}dx &amp;=\sqrt{1+\left[{@dy@}\right]^{2}}dx \\
    &amp;=\sqrt{1+{@dy^2@}}\,dx \\
    &amp;={@(factor(sqrt(1+dy^2))),simp@}\,dx
    \end{align}
    \)
</p>
<p>Since the limits of integration are given in \(y\) we then convert them in terms of \(x\) as shown below:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle y_{1}={@lower_y@}: \qquad {@lower_y@}={@rhs(expr)@} \qquad \rightarrow x_{1}={@rhs_x1@}\)</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  y_{2}={@upper_y@}: \qquad {@upper_y@}={@rhs(expr)@} \qquad \rightarrow x_{2}={@rhs_x2@}\)</p>
<p>So, the corresponding ranges of \(x's\) is \(\displaystyle  {@rhs_x1@}\leq x\leq{@rhs_x2@}\)</p>
<p>The integral giving the arc length is then, </p>
<p>
    \[
    \begin{align}
    \displaystyle L &amp;= \int ds \\
\displaystyle     &amp;= \int_{{@rhs_x1@}}^{{@rhs_x2@}} {@(factor(sqrt(1+dy^2))),simp@}\,dx \\
\displaystyle     &amp;= \left[ {@int((factor(sqrt(1+dy^2))),x)@} \right]_{{@rhs_x1@}}^{{@rhs_x2@}} \\
\displaystyle     &amp;= {@arc_length@} \\
\displaystyle     &amp;= {@ta@}
    \end{align}
    \]
</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
